      <p class="eyebrow">Second lien strategy</p>
      <h2>How a HELOC fits behind your first mortgage</h2>
      <p>Lenders look at your combined loan-to-value: everything you owe against the home divided by its value. Many HELOC programs go up to about 90%.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="lien"><div class="tag">1st</div><div><h3>Your current mortgage</h3><p>Stays in place with its existing terms. Nothing about it changes.</p></div></div>
      <div class="lien second"><div class="tag">2nd</div><div><h3>Your new HELOC</h3><p>A separate line in second position. Draw during the draw period, then repay over the repayment period.</p></div></div>
      <div class="lien"><div class="tag num">90%</div><div><h3>The ceiling</h3><p>First mortgage plus HELOC can typically reach up to 90% of the home's value. The gap between what you owe and that 90% mark is your estimated line.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="band"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Questions homeowners ask</p>
      <h2>HELOC answers in plain English</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>How much can I borrow with a HELOC?</summary><p>A common starting point is 90% of your home's value minus your first mortgage balance. On a $650,000 home with $310,000 owed, that is $585,000 − $310,000 = $275,000. Your exact line depends on appraisal, credit, income and program guidelines.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does this HELOC check pull my credit?</summary><p>No. The estimate uses only the two numbers you type in. Credit is reviewed later, only if you decide to move forward with a loan officer.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How fast can a HELOC close?</summary><p>On our fast-track HELOC, eligible homeowners can close in as few as 7 days. Most of the time savings comes from connecting your bank electronically and using an automated valuation instead of an appraisal. Title or property issues can add time.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need an appraisal or tax returns for this HELOC?</summary><p>Most homes qualify for an automated valuation, so no in-person appraisal. Income is verified from your connected bank statements, so most borrowers skip tax returns and pay stubs. If your file needs more, your loan officer will tell you up front.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Will a HELOC change my first mortgage?</summary><p>No. A HELOC is a separate second lien. Your first mortgage keeps its balance, terms and servicer.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What is the difference between a HELOC and a cash-out refinance?</summary><p>A cash-out refinance replaces your first mortgage with a new, larger loan. A HELOC leaves the first mortgage alone and adds a line you can draw from over time. A loan officer can lay out both side by side for your situation.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What can I use HELOC money for?</summary><p>Home improvements, paying off other balances, emergencies, education, a down payment on another property, or business needs. Your home secures the line, so borrow with a plan for repayment.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Who will contact me after I check?</summary><p>Usually Drew or Alisha on Jim Blackburn's team calls or texts first to confirm your details and set up a time. Your loan options are reviewed with Jim, a licensed loan officer (NMLS #1072866) at Stairway Mortgage. There is no obligation.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div></section>

